Coastal wetlands, including marshes, mangroves, and swamps, act as natural sponges and shock absorbers, significantly mitigating the destructive power of storm surges and high winds. Their ability to reduce flood damage is not merely aesthetic but rooted in distinct physical mechanisms that interact with moving water and air.

